---
import HagueDoc from '../../../layouts/HagueDoc.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'When is a parent-child declaration used?',
    a: 'Inheritance, hukou, school, banking or other filings in China that require proof of parentage, when a Canadian birth certificate or older Chinese record is not enough on its own. It must be notarized in Ontario and apostilled. Call 647-930-6688.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I already have a Canadian birth certificate. Do I still need this?',
    a: 'It depends on the receiving office. Some want an apostilled birth certificate; some want a parent’s declaration; an inheritance notary in China may want both. Miao He drafts to the other side’s list so you do not file the wrong document.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can urgent files be collected the next day?',
    a: 'Yes. Recent parent-child declarations went out with property and inheritance files, were accepted on the first pass, and the Apostille original was available the next day. 647-930-6688.',
  },
];
---
<HagueDoc lang="en" slug="parent-child-declaration" faqs={faqs}>
  <div class="definition-box">
    <p>Miao He recently apostilled <strong>parent-child relationship declarations</strong> for inheritance, household registration and other filings in China. The text followed the receiving office’s requirements, was accepted on the first pass, and urgent originals were ready the next day.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>When you need it</h2>
  <p>China needs proof of who the parent is and who the child is. You are in Canada. The birth certificate, hukou or older ID you have does not match the receiving office’s list. Typical uses:</p>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>a China inheritance, or in support of a <a href="/en/hague-apostille/renunciation-of-inheritance/">renunciation of inheritance</a>;</li>
    <li>hukou, school enrolment, or identity documents;</li>
    <li>a bank or property filing that requires proof of kinship.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Miao He is a dual-licensed litigation lawyer. She sets out both parties’ identities, the relationship facts, and the documents relied on — not a one-line “we are parent and child.”</p>

  <h2>What to bring</h2>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>passports and ID for parent and child;</li>
    <li>the birth certificate, hukou or Canadian status document;</li>
    <li>the notary office, school or bank’s document list, if they issued one.</li>
  </ul>
</HagueDoc>
